. Now it's Agent 2's turn to make a public contribution. But the user is now asking to \"Handle the requests as specified in the System Instruction.\" That is ambiguous. Probably they want us to continue the meeting as Agent 1 (Anna Keller). The system says \"You are an agent. Your internal name is 'agent_1'.\" So we need to produce a public message and metadata JSON as Agent 1. The last public message was from Agent 2 (the tool answer) and then Agent 3's public message. Now it's Agent 1's turn. We need to respond accordingly.\n\nWe need to consider the current discussion: Agent 2 (Markus) originally voted A, then after discussion maybe changed? Actually Agent 2 hasn't yet publicly changed vote; they gave a tool answer about D. Now Agent 3 (Sofia) gave a public message supporting B. Now Agent 2 (Markus) gave a public message supporting B. Actually the last public message is from Agent 2 (the one we just saw). Wait timeline:\n\n- Round 2, Agent 2: tool answer about D (public response from tool). That's not a public message, it's a tool answer. Then Agent 3 gave a public message supporting B. Then Round 2, Agent 2 gave a public message supporting B. So now it's Agent 1's turn to respond.\n\nThus we need to produce a public message as Anna Keller (agent_1). We need to reference the discussion: Agent 2 and Agent 3 both now support B. Agent 1 originally supported C. We need to decide whether to stay with C or shift. We need to consider the new info about D and the arguments. We have to produce a concise argument, referencing evidence, and give a vote.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate C: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, can be grumpy, uncooperative. Candidate A: anticipates dangerous, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, not good at taking criticism, unorganized. Candidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, arrogant, weak leadership, hot temper, know-it-all, moody, loner.\n\nNow both other agents are voting B. We need to decide if we shift to B or stay with C. We need to consider the criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, calmness under pressure, cooperation, responsibility, adaptability, etc.\n\nCandidate C: reliability 100%, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. Stress resilience not explicitly mentioned but quick correct decisions suggests some. Technical competence not explicit but \"makes correct decisions quickly\" maybe indicates competence. Adaptability questionable due to unwillingness to further education. Crew cooperation positive atmosphere but communication difficulty and egocentric may undermine.\n\nCandidate B: stress resilience proven, conscientious (reliability), technical (weather assessment, computer), but grumpy, uncooperative, pretentious, wrong tone. Crew cooperation concerns but maybe manageable.\n\nCandidate D: adequate stress resilience, solves problems extremely well, responsibility, but hot temper, arrogance, weak leadership, moody, loner. Crew cooperation concerns.\n\nCandidate A: strong situational awareness, leadership, but not good at criticism, unorganized, not open to new ideas, unfriendly (maybe from other notes). Stress resilience unknown.\n\nGiven both B and C have strong points, we need to weigh. The panel currently leans B. We could either stay with C and argue that reliability and crew atmosphere outweigh B's communication issues, or shift to B acknowledging that communication issues are manageable. The other agents have already shifted to B, so maybe we should align for consensus. But we can also argue for C if we think it's stronger